{0 Deriving Yaml}

This ppx is based on {{: https://github.com/NathanReb/ppx_yojson} ppx_yojson}
and {{: https://github.com/ocaml-ppx/ppx_deriving_yojson} ppx_deriving_yojson}
because of the many similarities between JSON and yaml.

{1 Basic Usage}

For converting OCaml values to Yaml values [ppx_deriving_yamlx] will do the
conventional dropping of the type name if it is [t]. Otherwise the type name is
the prefix to the [to_yamlx] function.

[to_yamlx] produces a {! YAMLx.value}.

[of_yamlx] produces OCaml types wrapped in a {! Stdlib.result}.

Here is a small example.

{@ocaml[
# type person = { name : string; age : int } [@@deriving yamlx]
type person = { name : string; age : int; }
val person_to_yamlx : person -> YAMLx.value = <fun>
val person_of_yamlx : YAMLx.value -> (person, [> `Msg of string ]) result =
  <fun>
# type users = person list [@@deriving yamlx]
type users = person list
val users_to_yamlx : person list -> YAMLx.value = <fun>
val users_of_yamlx : YAMLx.value -> (person list, [> `Msg of string ]) result =
  <fun>
]}

This will produce four functions, a [_to_yamlx] and [_of_yamlx] for both a person and
the users. For example:

{@ocaml[
# person_to_yamlx;;
- : person -> YAMLx.value = <fun>
# users_of_yamlx;;
- : YAMLx.value -> (person list, [> `Msg of string ]) result = <fun>
]}

If your type constructors have arguments, then the functions will be
higher-order and you will need to supply a function to convert values for each
constructor argument. For example:

{@ocaml[
type 'a note = { txt : 'a } [@@deriving yamlx]
]}

produces the following function.

{@ocaml[
# note_to_yamlx;;
- : ('a -> YAMLx.value) -> 'a note -> YAMLx.value = <fun>
]}

Finally, if you only need the encoder ([to_yamlx]) or the decoder ([of_yamlx]) then there are single versions of the deriver for those.

{@ocaml[
# type x = { age : int }[@@deriving to_yamlx];;
type x = { age : int; }
val x_to_yamlx : x -> YAMLx.value = <fun>
]}

{1 Attributes}

{2 Key and Name}

Record field names cannot begin with a capital letter and variant constructors
must start with one. This limits what the generated Yaml can look like. To
override the Yaml names you can use the [[@key <string>]] and [[@name
<string>]] attributes for records and variants respectively.

{@ocaml[
# type t = {
    camel_name : string [@key "camel-name"]
  }[@@deriving to_yamlx];;
type t = { camel_name : string; }
val to_yamlx : t -> YAMLx.value = <fun>

# YAMLx.Value.to_yaml (to_yamlx { camel_name = "Alice" });;
- : string = "camel-name: Alice\n"
]}

{2 Default Values}

You can also specify default values for fields.

{@ocaml[
type t = {
  name : string;
  age : int [@default 42]
}[@@deriving yamlx]
]}

These will be used in the absence of any fields when decoding Yaml values into OCaml ones.

{@ocaml[
# YAMLx.Value.of_yaml_exn "name: Alice" |> of_yamlx;;
- : (t, [> `Msg of string ]) result = Ok {name = "Alice"; age = 42}
]}

{2 Custom encoding and decoding}

Sometimes you might want to specify your own encoding and decoding logic on field
by field basis. To do so, you can use the [of_yamlx] and [to_yamlx] attributes.

{@ocaml[
type t = {
  name : string [@to_yamlx fun i -> YAMLx.String (YAMLx.zero_loc, ("custom-" ^ i))]
}[@@deriving yamlx]
]}

The [to_yamlx] function will use the custom encoder now instead.

{@ocaml[
# YAMLx.Value.to_yaml (to_yamlx { name = "alice" });;
- : string = "name: custom-alice\n"
]}

{1 Partially Decoding}

There is a [~skip_unknown] flag for telling the deriver to simply ignore any
fields which are missing. This is particularly useful when you only wish to
partially decode a yaml value.

Consider the following yaml:

{@ocaml[
let yaml = "name: Bob\nage: 42\nmisc: We don't need this!"
]}

If we try to do the normal decoding of this but only partially extract the fields, it will throw an error.

{@ocaml[
# type t = {
    name : string;
    age : int;
  }[@@deriving of_yamlx];;
type t = { name : string; age : int; }
val of_yamlx : YAMLx.value -> (t, [> `Msg of string ]) result = <fun>

# YAMLx.Value.of_yaml_exn yaml |> of_yamlx;;
- : (t, [> `Msg of string ]) result =
Error (`Msg "Failed to find the case for: misc")
]}

Instead we tell the deriver to ignore unknown fields.

{@ocaml[
type t = {
  name : string;
  age : int;
}[@@deriving of_yamlx ~skip_unknown]
]}

{@ocaml[
# YAMLx.Value.of_yaml_exn yaml |> of_yamlx;;
- : (t, [> `Msg of string ]) result = Ok {name = "Bob"; age = 42}
]}

{1 Implementation Details}

One important thing is that ['a option] values within records will return [None] if the Yaml you are trying to convert does not exist.

{table
{tr {th OCaml Type}{th Yamlx Type}}
{tr {td [int]}{td {! YAMLx.Int}}}
{tr {td [float]}{td {! YAMLx.Float}}}
{tr {td [string]}{td {! YAMLx.String}}}
{tr {td [bool]}{td {! YAMLx.Bool }}}
{tr {td [None]}{td {! YAMLx.Null }}}
{tr {td [list]}{td {! YAMLx.Seq}}}
{tr {td [array]}{td {! YAMLx.Seq}}}
{tr {td [record] e.g [{ name : string }]}{td [YAMLx.Map [(_, YAMLx.String (_ ,"name"), YAMLx.String (_, s))]]}}
{tr {td [A of int] or [ [`A of int]]}{td [YAMLx.Map [(_, YAMLx.String (_, "A"), YAMLx.Seq (_, [ YAMLx.Float (_, f)]))]]}}
}
